56
00:02:10,810 --> 00:02:13,390
So what is
hypersensitivity?

57
00:02:14,170 --> 00:02:16,430
Hypersensitivity
is an excessive or

58
00:02:16,430 --> 00:02:19,090
inappropriate immune
response to benign

59
00:02:19,090 --> 00:02:21,350
foreign antigens
that causes damage.

60
00:02:21,710 --> 00:02:24,970
The immune response,
not the antigen,

61
00:02:24,970 --> 00:02:27,070
is the harmful
part to the body.

62
00:02:27,130 --> 00:02:29,160
This definition is quite

63
00:02:29,160 --> 00:02:31,690
oversimplified,
so we are going to

64
00:02:31,690 --> 00:02:33,870
go into it a bit
more in depth.

65
00:02:34,710 --> 00:02:37,350
Hypersensitivity
reactions are reliant on

66
00:02:37,350 --> 00:02:40,130
the adaptive immune
system, and previous

67
00:02:40,130 --> 00:02:43,510
exposure to the antigen
is required to prime the

68
00:02:43,510 --> 00:02:45,850
adaptive immune
response to produce the

69
00:02:45,850 --> 00:02:50,010
respective antibody.
That is, hypersensitivity

70
00:02:50,010 --> 00:02:53,390
reactions do not take
place when an individual

71
00:02:53,390 --> 00:02:56,990
is first exposed to
an allergen, which

72
00:02:56,990 --> 00:02:59,850
is an extremely common
misconception and an

73
00:02:59,850 --> 00:03:02,290
important point to get
across to patients.

74
00:03:03,650 --> 00:03:05,530
Hypersensitivity
occurs in response

75
00:03:05,530 --> 00:03:07,930
to two general
types of antigens,

76
00:03:08,090 --> 00:03:10,970
foreign antigens
like microbes and

77
00:03:10,970 --> 00:03:14,090
environmental agents,
and self-antigens.

78
00:03:14,670 --> 00:03:17,090
Responses to self
-antigens are a

79
00:03:17,090 --> 00:03:19,510
result from failure
of self-tolerance.

80
00:03:19,890 --> 00:03:22,070
Tolerance is an
important facet

81
00:03:22,070 --> 00:03:23,850
because it keeps our
immune system from

82
00:03:23,850 --> 00:03:26,090
attacking itself,
but it doesn't

83
00:03:26,090 --> 00:03:28,250
function perfectly
in all individuals.

84
00:03:28,430 --> 00:03:31,030
This is what we
call autoimmunity.

85
00:03:33,190 --> 00:03:36,290
The system of hypersensitivity
classification

86
00:03:36,290 --> 00:03:38,230
that we use in
the medical field

87
00:03:38,230 --> 00:03:41,230
was first described
by Gell and Coombs and

88
00:03:41,230 --> 00:03:43,450
is based on the
principal immunologic

89
00:03:43,450 --> 00:03:46,110
mechanism responsible
for tissue injury.

90
00:03:46,430 --> 00:03:49,070
Each type of
reaction is antigen

91
00:03:49,070 --> 00:03:50,710
-specific and produces a

92
00:03:50,710 --> 00:03:52,730
characteristic
clinical disease.

93
00:03:53,000 --> 00:03:55,590
We are going to review
each of the four

94
00:03:55,590 --> 00:03:58,530
types of hypersensitivity
reactions, and

95
00:03:58,530 --> 00:04:00,530
this slide demonstrates
a helpful memory

96
00:04:00,530 --> 00:04:02,730
aid to keep the
numbers and mechanisms

97
00:04:02,730 --> 00:04:05,970
straight in your head
type 1 is known as

98
00:04:05,970 --> 00:04:09,070
immediate hypersensitivity
type 2 is known

99
00:04:09,070 --> 00:04:12,230
as antibody mediated
type 3 is known

100
00:04:12,230 --> 00:04:16,030
as immune complex
mediated and type 4 is

101
00:04:16,030 --> 00:04:19,730
known as delayed type
hypersensitivity we

102
00:04:19,730 --> 00:04:21,770
will review this
slide again once we

103
00:04:21,770 --> 00:04:24,450
discuss each type to
solidify the concepts

104
00:04:26,450 --> 00:04:30,290
Type 1 hypersensitivity,
also called

105
00:04:30,290 --> 00:04:34,970
immediate hypersensitivity,
is an IgE antibody

106
00:04:34,970 --> 00:04:37,990
and mass cell
mediated reaction to

107
00:04:37,990 --> 00:04:40,670
certain antigens that
causes rapid vascular

108
00:04:40,670 --> 00:04:44,370
leakage, excessive
mucosal secretions, and

109
00:04:44,370 --> 00:04:46,730
contraction of
bronchial and intestinal

110
00:04:46,730 --> 00:04:49,570
smooth muscle, followed
by inflammation.

111
00:04:52,890 --> 00:04:55,650
The sequence of
events and development

112
00:04:55,650 --> 00:04:57,530
of immediate
hypersensitivity

113
00:04:57,530 --> 00:04:59,770
has a few steps
which are illustrated

114
00:04:59,770 --> 00:05:01,550
by the diagram
on the right.

115
00:05:01,570 --> 00:05:04,770
It looks quite complex,
so I'm going to

116
00:05:04,770 --> 00:05:07,730
break it down into a
few more simpler steps.

117
00:05:07,970 --> 00:05:11,050
During sensitization,
the antigen

118
00:05:11,050 --> 00:05:13,990
activates T
helper 2 cells and

119
00:05:13,990 --> 00:05:16,890
follicular helper T
cells, which secrete

120
00:05:16,890 --> 00:05:19,710
interleukin-4 and
interleukin-13,

121
00:05:19,830 --> 00:05:23,830
which then stimulate
production of IgE and

122
00:05:23,830 --> 00:05:26,550
facilitate its binding
to its receptor on

123
00:05:26,550 --> 00:05:30,430
mast cells. With subsequent
antigen exposure,

124
00:05:30,610 --> 00:05:33,430
immediate hypersensitivity
reaction takes

125
00:05:33,430 --> 00:05:38,470
place. The antigen
crosslinks preformed IgE

126
00:05:38,470 --> 00:05:41,310
on sensitized mast
cells and triggers

127
00:05:41,310 --> 00:05:45,790
degranulation. This
then releases
various mediators,

128
00:05:45,790 --> 00:05:48,370
a few of which I
will briefly mention.

129
00:05:49,030 --> 00:05:51,750
Histamine causes
increased vascular

130
00:05:51,750 --> 00:05:55,330
permeability and vasodilation,
leading to leakage

131
00:05:55,330 --> 00:05:58,010
of fluid and plasma
proteins into tissues

132
00:05:58,010 --> 00:06:00,910
and stimulates the
transient contraction

133
00:06:00,910 --> 00:06:03,690
of bronchial and
intestinal smooth muscle.

134
00:06:04,330 --> 00:06:07,270
Proteases may damage
local tissues.

135
00:06:07,910 --> 00:06:10,850
Prostaglandins cause
vascular dilation.

136
00:06:11,630 --> 00:06:14,070
Leukotrienes
stimulate prolonged

137
00:06:14,070 --> 00:06:16,030
bronchial smooth
muscle contraction.

138
00:06:16,410 --> 00:06:20,010
This is referred to
as the early, acute,

139
00:06:20,010 --> 00:06:22,830
or immediate phase
of an immediate

140
00:06:22,830 --> 00:06:26,150
hypersensitivity reaction.
These all mean the

141
00:06:26,150 --> 00:06:28,730
same thing and are
used interchangeably.

142
00:06:29,350 --> 00:06:32,150
Other mast cell
mediators are also

143
00:06:32,150 --> 00:06:34,250
released but play
a different role.

144
00:06:34,990 --> 00:06:36,650
Cytokines stimulate the

145
00:06:36,650 --> 00:06:38,570
recruitment of
eosinophils,

146
00:06:38,730 --> 00:06:41,550
neutrophils, and
T-helper 2 cells,

147
00:06:41,550 --> 00:06:44,070
which are primarily
responsible for

148
00:06:44,070 --> 00:06:46,450
the late or delayed
phase reaction.

149
00:06:46,990 --> 00:06:48,890
This phase's
hallmarks are

150
00:06:48,890 --> 00:06:50,850
inflammation and
tissue damage.

151
00:06:51,310 --> 00:06:54,690
Mass cell-derived TNF and

152
00:06:54,690 --> 00:06:57,070
interleukin-4
promote inflammation.

153
00:06:57,310 --> 00:07:01,450
Interleukin-5 activates
eosinophils, which,

154
00:07:01,450 --> 00:07:04,170
along with neutrophils,
release proteases

155
00:07:04,170 --> 00:07:07,590
and cause tissue damage.
Because immediate

156
00:07:07,590 --> 00:07:09,890
hypersensitivity
reactions have the

157
00:07:09,890 --> 00:07:12,710
capability to cause
two symptom flares, so

158
00:07:12,710 --> 00:07:15,710
to speak, from a single
antigen exposure,

159
00:07:15,830 --> 00:07:19,010
we refer to it as
causing biphasic

160
00:07:19,010 --> 00:07:21,130
reactions, meaning
two phases.

161
00:07:21,830 --> 00:07:24,870
Type I hypersensitivities
include

162
00:07:24,870 --> 00:07:27,610
atopic diseases
such as asthma,

163
00:07:27,670 --> 00:07:29,090
allergic rhinitis,

164
00:07:29,250 --> 00:07:30,870
allergic conjunctivitis,

165
00:07:30,870 --> 00:07:32,750
and atopic dermatitis.

166
00:07:32,970 --> 00:07:36,670
Type I hypersensitivities
also include allergic

167
00:07:36,670 --> 00:07:40,510
diseases such as
anaphylaxis, urticaria,

168
00:07:40,510 --> 00:07:44,230
and angioedema. It is
very important to note

169
00:07:44,230 --> 00:07:48,250
that type 2 and type
3 hypersensitivity

170
00:07:48,250 --> 00:07:52,050
reactions can also
result in an anaphylactic

171
00:07:52,050 --> 00:07:55,990
reaction within 24 hours
of antigen exposure.

172
00:07:58,950 --> 00:08:02,830
Type II hypersensitivity
reactions are

173
00:08:02,830 --> 00:08:07,350
caused by IgG and IgM
antibodies against

174
00:08:07,350 --> 00:08:10,030
cells or extracellular
matrix components

175
00:08:10,030 --> 00:08:12,730
which deposit in
any tissue that

176
00:08:12,730 --> 00:08:15,150
expresses the relevant
target antigen.

177
00:08:15,570 --> 00:08:17,990
This results in the
hallmark cellular

178
00:08:17,990 --> 00:08:21,170
destruction,
inflammation, and cellular

179
00:08:21,170 --> 00:08:23,070
dysfunction seen
in these disorders.

180
00:08:23,070 --> 00:08:26,590
Type 2 hypersensitivity
disorders,

181
00:08:26,590 --> 00:08:29,170
primarily due to
cellular destruction,

182
00:08:29,290 --> 00:08:32,130
also termed
antibody-dependent

183
00:08:32,130 --> 00:08:33,890
cellular cytotoxicity,

184
00:08:34,010 --> 00:08:36,970
are autoimmune
hemolytic anemia,

185
00:08:37,270 --> 00:08:39,830
immune thrombocytopenia,

186
00:08:40,090 --> 00:08:42,690
transfusion
reactions, and

187
00:08:42,690 --> 00:08:44,730
hemolytic disease
of the newborn.

188
00:08:45,250 --> 00:08:48,150
Examples of disorders
due to inflammation

189
00:08:48,150 --> 00:08:51,250
are Good-Pasture
syndrome, which is

190
00:08:51,250 --> 00:08:53,230
a renal disease you
will learn about

191
00:08:53,230 --> 00:08:55,210
in your second year
of medical school,

192
00:08:55,470 --> 00:08:58,590
rheumatic fever,
and hyperacute

193
00:08:58,590 --> 00:08:59,850
transplant rejection.

194
00:09:00,350 --> 00:09:01,510
Finally,

195
00:09:01,790 --> 00:09:04,170
cellular dysfunction
caused by type 2

196
00:09:04,170 --> 00:09:07,970
hypersensitivity results
in myasthenia gravis,

197
00:09:08,050 --> 00:09:11,570
Graves disease, and
pentagus vulgaris.

198
00:09:11,790 --> 00:09:15,130
So, I just threw a
ton of disease names

199
00:09:15,130 --> 00:09:17,650
at you, which are also
listed on the next

200
00:09:17,650 --> 00:09:20,230
slide. But how do
they actually occur?

201
00:09:20,440 --> 00:09:21,750
Let's check it out.

202
00:09:24,830 --> 00:09:28,770
Bound IgG interacts
with COMPLIMA and

203
00:09:28,770 --> 00:09:31,730
with the FC receptor
of macrophages.

204
00:09:31,990 --> 00:09:34,190
Starting with panel A of

205
00:09:34,190 --> 00:09:35,650
the diagram on the right,

206
00:09:35,810 --> 00:09:39,250
type 2 hypersensitivity
can occur when

207
00:09:39,250 --> 00:09:42,690
IgG antibodies cross
-link with antigens.

208
00:09:42,690 --> 00:09:45,490
Then they bond to
neutrophils and

209
00:09:45,490 --> 00:09:48,290
macrophage FC
receptors and activate

210
00:09:48,290 --> 00:09:52,200
them, which results
in inflammation. Both

211
00:09:52,200 --> 00:09:56,750
IgG and IgM, when
bound to antigens,

212
00:09:56,970 --> 00:09:59,310
activate the classical
pathway of the

213
00:09:59,310 --> 00:10:01,870
complement system and
produce inflammation.

214
00:10:02,170 --> 00:10:05,770
When leukocytes are
activated at the

215
00:10:05,770 --> 00:10:08,330
site of antibody
deposition, they

216
00:10:08,330 --> 00:10:11,350
release reactive
oxygen species and

217
00:10:11,350 --> 00:10:13,970
lysosomal enzymes
that damage tissues.

218
00:10:14,210 --> 00:10:18,030
Panel B illustrates
opsonization and

219
00:10:18,030 --> 00:10:21,770
phagocytosis, two very
high-yield concepts

220
00:10:21,770 --> 00:10:24,390
when understanding
mechanisms of disease.

221
00:10:24,530 --> 00:10:28,250
When antibodies bind
to antigens on cells,

222
00:10:28,250 --> 00:10:32,050
the cells are opsonized,
or tagged, to make

223
00:10:32,050 --> 00:10:34,670
them more easily
recognized so they can be

224
00:10:34,670 --> 00:10:37,810
ingested and destroyed
by host phagocytes.

225
00:10:37,810 --> 00:10:41,970
When an erythrocyte or
platelet is opsonized,

226
00:10:41,970 --> 00:10:45,450
we can see autoimmune
hemolytic anemia,

227
00:10:45,870 --> 00:10:48,150
immune thrombocytopenia,

228
00:10:48,450 --> 00:10:50,590
transfusion
reactions, and

229
00:10:50,590 --> 00:10:52,610
hemolytic disease
of the newborn.

230
00:10:53,450 --> 00:10:55,650
Panel C illustrates the

231
00:10:55,650 --> 00:10:57,630
abnormal cellular
responses.

232
00:10:58,390 --> 00:11:01,370
Antibodies can directly
activate receptors

233
00:11:01,370 --> 00:11:03,870
and mimic their
physiologic ligands,

234
00:11:03,870 --> 00:11:06,790
such is with Graves'
disease, a form

235
00:11:06,790 --> 00:11:09,690
of antibody-mediated
hyperthyroidism.

236
00:11:10,250 --> 00:11:13,630
Antibodies can also
block receptors, such

237
00:11:13,630 --> 00:11:16,210
as in myasthenia
gravis, when these

238
00:11:16,210 --> 00:11:19,630
autoantibodies block
nicotinic acetylcholine

239
00:11:19,630 --> 00:11:22,950
receptors and prevent
muscle cells from receiving

240
00:11:22,950 --> 00:11:25,390
signals from motor
neurons to contract,

241
00:11:25,390 --> 00:11:28,390
resulting and muscle
weakness and fatigue.

242
00:11:31,750 --> 00:11:36,630
Type 3 hypersensitivity
involves IgG,

243
00:11:36,750 --> 00:11:40,870
IgM, and sometimes
IgA antibodies

244
00:11:40,870 --> 00:11:43,950
forming immune
complexes with antigen.

245
00:11:44,170 --> 00:11:47,110
These complexes are
actually normal.

246
00:11:47,500 --> 00:11:50,230
Type 3 hypersensitivity
reactions

247
00:11:50,230 --> 00:11:53,070
are prevented
by activation of

248
00:11:53,070 --> 00:11:54,990
the classical
complement pathway,

249
00:11:55,200 --> 00:11:59,170
especially C3, which
inserts itself and

250
00:11:59,170 --> 00:12:01,850
breaks up the lattice
of the immune complex.

251
00:12:02,190 --> 00:12:05,030
Red blood cells
also transfer these

252
00:12:05,030 --> 00:12:07,290
immune complexes
from tissues in

253
00:12:07,290 --> 00:12:10,430
blood using complement
receptor 1, the

254
00:12:10,430 --> 00:12:13,370
receptor for C3,
to phagocytes of

255
00:12:13,370 --> 00:12:15,290
the liver and spleen
for breakdown.

256
00:12:16,280 --> 00:12:19,450
However, problems
arise when immune

257
00:12:19,450 --> 00:12:22,470
complexes are formed
in excessive amounts,

258
00:12:22,470 --> 00:12:24,870
are not efficiently
removed by

259
00:12:24,870 --> 00:12:28,130
phagocytes and become
deposited in tissues.

260
00:12:28,530 --> 00:12:31,590
Immune complexes
usually deposit in

261
00:12:31,590 --> 00:12:34,550
blood vessels and
present as vasculitis.

262
00:12:34,750 --> 00:12:38,750
Once deposited in
vessel walls, the FC

263
00:12:38,750 --> 00:12:41,590
regions of antibodies
activate complement

264
00:12:41,590 --> 00:12:44,230
and bind neutrophils
to induce release

265
00:12:44,230 --> 00:12:47,750
of proteases and
reactive oxygen species.

266
00:12:48,190 --> 00:12:51,450
This inflammatory
response within the vessel

267
00:12:51,450 --> 00:12:54,110
wall may cause
local hemorrhage or

268
00:12:54,110 --> 00:12:57,510
thrombosis leading to
ischemic tissue injury.

269
00:12:57,870 --> 00:12:59,690
So, to reiterate,

270
00:13:00,210 --> 00:13:02,050
three things
stick together to

271
00:13:02,050 --> 00:13:03,950
cause a type 3
hypersensitivity.

272
00:13:04,790 --> 00:13:08,190
Antibody, antigen,
and complement.

273
00:13:11,070 --> 00:13:14,690
Type 3 hypersensitivity
reactions can be

274
00:13:14,690 --> 00:13:18,630
local, called an ARTHIS
reaction, or disseminated,

275
00:13:18,630 --> 00:13:21,910
called serum
sickness. The first

276
00:13:21,910 --> 00:13:25,190
immune complex disease
studied was serum sickness

277
00:13:25,190 --> 00:13:27,810
and was observed
when human subjects

278
00:13:27,810 --> 00:13:31,250
received antitoxin
-containing serum from

279
00:13:31,250 --> 00:13:34,050
immunized animals for
treatment of infection.

280
00:13:34,210 --> 00:13:37,190
Some of these
subjects developed a

281
00:13:37,190 --> 00:13:40,610
systemic inflammatory
disease with fever,

282
00:13:40,670 --> 00:13:44,410
rash, and arthritis
caused by antibodies

283
00:13:44,410 --> 00:13:47,110
made against injected
animal proteins,

284
00:13:47,110 --> 00:13:49,470
which then formed
complexes with

285
00:13:49,470 --> 00:13:52,330
these proteins and
deposited in tissues.

286
00:13:52,450 --> 00:13:56,190
This phenomenon was
replicated experimentally

287
00:13:56,190 --> 00:13:59,670
to confirm this mechanism
and can occur as

288
00:13:59,670 --> 00:14:02,770
a complication of
any therapy involving

289
00:14:02,770 --> 00:14:06,350
injection of foreign
proteins, such as antibodies

290
00:14:06,350 --> 00:14:09,790
against microbial
toxins, snake venoms,

291
00:14:10,030 --> 00:14:13,030
animal-derived T
-cells, or humanized

292
00:14:13,030 --> 00:14:15,530
monoclonal antibodies
that are commonly

293
00:14:15,530 --> 00:14:17,810
used nowadays to
treat disease.

294
00:14:18,070 --> 00:14:21,570
Examples of serum
sickness like type 3

295
00:14:21,570 --> 00:14:26,030
hypersensitivity reactions
are systemic lupus

296
00:14:26,030 --> 00:14:30,010
erythematosus,
colloquially called lupus,

297
00:14:30,290 --> 00:14:32,070
rheumatoid arthritis,

298
00:14:32,630 --> 00:14:35,570
post-streptococcal
glomerulonephritis,

299
00:14:35,970 --> 00:14:39,690
infections like
hepatitis B, and some

300
00:14:39,690 --> 00:14:42,970
drugs which act as
haptons like penicillin.

301
00:14:43,310 --> 00:14:46,970
In contrast, a
localized immune

302
00:14:46,970 --> 00:14:49,510
complex reaction
was first studied in

303
00:14:49,510 --> 00:14:52,770
animals and was
induced by subcutaneous

304
00:14:52,770 --> 00:14:55,150
administration of
a protein antigen

305
00:14:55,150 --> 00:14:57,870
to a previously
immunized animal.

306
00:14:58,400 --> 00:15:00,730
This injection
resulted in the

307
00:15:00,730 --> 00:15:03,290
formation of
immune complexes at

308
00:15:03,290 --> 00:15:05,390
the site of
antigen injection

309
00:15:05,440 --> 00:15:09,670
and resulted in a
local vasculitis. A

310
00:15:09,670 --> 00:15:12,550
classic example of
localized type 3

311
00:15:12,550 --> 00:15:16,090
hypersensitivity reaction
is hypersensitivity

312
00:15:16,090 --> 00:15:19,170
pneumonitis, also
called farmer's lung.

313
00:15:21,570 --> 00:15:24,810
Type 4 hypersensitivity
reactions

314
00:15:24,810 --> 00:15:27,450
are unique because
they are caused

315
00:15:27,450 --> 00:15:29,830
by T lymphocytes
rather than

316
00:15:30,040 --> 00:15:32,030
immunoglobulins like
the other three.

317
00:15:32,210 --> 00:15:35,510
These reactions
result either from

318
00:15:35,510 --> 00:15:37,770
inflammation
caused by cytokines

319
00:15:37,770 --> 00:15:42,390
produced by CD4, T
helper 1, and T helper

320
00:15:42,390 --> 00:15:45,590
17 cells, or by
killing of host

321
00:15:45,590 --> 00:15:49,350
cells by CD8 cytotoxic
T lymphocytes.

322
00:15:50,090 --> 00:15:54,010
CD4 T cells secrete
cytokines that induce

323
00:15:54,010 --> 00:15:57,190
local inflammation and
activate macrophages.

324
00:15:57,470 --> 00:16:00,050
Different diseases
may be associated

325
00:16:00,050 --> 00:16:02,970
with activation
of T helper 1 and

326
00:16:02,970 --> 00:16:06,930
T helper 17 cells.
T helper 1 cells

327
00:16:06,930 --> 00:16:09,290
are the source of
interferon gamma,

328
00:16:09,410 --> 00:16:11,870
the principal
macrophage activating

329
00:16:11,870 --> 00:16:15,590
cytokine, and T
-helper 17 cells are

330
00:16:15,590 --> 00:16:17,430
responsible for
the recruitment of

331
00:16:17,430 --> 00:16:19,570
leukocytes, including
neutrophils.

332
00:16:19,810 --> 00:16:23,230
Other diseases are
associated with direct

333
00:16:23,230 --> 00:16:27,910
cell cytotoxicity via
CD8 T-cells, which

334
00:16:27,910 --> 00:16:30,730
produce cytokines
like interferon gamma.

335
00:16:33,650 --> 00:16:37,630
Type 4 hypersensitivity
is also unique

336
00:16:37,630 --> 00:16:40,930
in that it occurs
24 to 48 hours

337
00:16:40,930 --> 00:16:44,530
after a sensitized
individual is exposed

338
00:16:44,530 --> 00:16:47,390
to the antigen,
which is why this

339
00:16:47,390 --> 00:16:49,870
type of hypersensitivity
is referred

340
00:16:49,870 --> 00:16:52,790
to as delayed type
hypersensitivity.

341
00:16:52,990 --> 00:16:56,650
This delay occurs
because it takes time for

342
00:16:56,650 --> 00:16:59,710
circulating effector
T cells to migrate to

343
00:16:59,710 --> 00:17:02,710
the site of antigen
challenge, respond to

344
00:17:02,710 --> 00:17:06,470
the antigen, and secrete
cytokines to induce

345
00:17:06,470 --> 00:17:09,070
a detectable reaction
in the individual.

346
00:17:09,500 --> 00:17:13,410
The major causes of
type 4 hypersensitivity

347
00:17:13,410 --> 00:17:16,410
reactions are
autoimmunity and

348
00:17:16,410 --> 00:17:19,270
exaggerated or
persistent responses to

349
00:17:19,270 --> 00:17:21,870
microbial or
environmental antigens.

350
00:17:22,250 --> 00:17:25,570
Examples include
contact sensitivity to

351
00:17:25,570 --> 00:17:28,770
chemicals, like
topical drugs, plants

352
00:17:28,770 --> 00:17:31,630
like poison ivy, and
metals like nickel,

353
00:17:31,630 --> 00:17:33,570
which is commonly
found in jewelry.

354
00:17:34,130 --> 00:17:37,690
In these situations,
the antigen binds to

355
00:17:37,690 --> 00:17:40,610
and modifies self
-proteins in the skin,

356
00:17:40,610 --> 00:17:43,170
creating new
antigens that

357
00:17:43,170 --> 00:17:45,150
can be recognized
by T-cells.

358
00:17:45,690 --> 00:17:48,730
The mechanism of type
4 hypersensitivity

359
00:17:48,730 --> 00:17:51,910
reactions is also
utilized to our advantage

360
00:17:51,910 --> 00:17:54,850
to determine if people
have been previously

361
00:17:54,850 --> 00:17:59,510
exposed to or respond
to an antigen. A

362
00:17:59,510 --> 00:18:03,890
classic example of this
is the PPD test, which

363
00:18:03,890 --> 00:18:06,230
stands for purified
protein derivative,

364
00:18:06,230 --> 00:18:09,530
which is injected
intradermally and in

365
00:18:09,530 --> 00:18:13,450
duration is measured 48
hours after to predict

366
00:18:13,450 --> 00:18:16,450
past or present
mycobacterial infection.

367
00:18:16,930 --> 00:18:19,340
An example of
an autoimmune

368
00:18:19,340 --> 00:18:21,210
disease that is
caused by a type

369
00:18:21,210 --> 00:18:23,170
4 hypersensitivity
reaction

370
00:18:23,190 --> 00:18:24,930
is type 1 diabetes,

371
00:18:25,050 --> 00:18:27,790
which is caused by
T cells responding

372
00:18:27,790 --> 00:18:31,130
to pancreatic islet
cell autoantigens,

373
00:18:31,650 --> 00:18:33,430
damaging islets
and eventually

374
00:18:33,430 --> 00:18:35,490
preventing
insulin secretion.

375
00:18:38,560 --> 00:18:41,010
Circling back to
the memory aid I

376
00:18:41,010 --> 00:18:43,330
introduced at the
beginning of this lecture,

377
00:18:43,330 --> 00:18:45,710
this should make
much more sense.

378
00:18:46,050 --> 00:18:48,270
Now that we have
talked about each type

379
00:18:48,270 --> 00:18:50,690
of hypersensitivity,
we are going

380
00:18:50,690 --> 00:18:53,590
to dive deeper into
a specific type of

381
00:18:53,590 --> 00:18:57,230
type 1 hypersensitivity,
anaphylaxis.

382
00:19:00,610 --> 00:19:03,650
Anaphylaxis is
defined as a serious

383
00:19:03,650 --> 00:19:05,810
allergic
hypersensitivity reaction

384
00:19:05,810 --> 00:19:08,810
that can progress
rapidly and may cause

385
00:19:08,810 --> 00:19:11,730
death. It may
involve the skin and

386
00:19:11,730 --> 00:19:14,210
mucosa, including
lips and tongue,

387
00:19:14,630 --> 00:19:16,890
respiratory,
cardiovascular,

388
00:19:16,890 --> 00:19:19,730
and or gastrointestinal
systems.

389
00:19:20,050 --> 00:19:23,190
Life-threatening
anaphylaxis is

390
00:19:23,190 --> 00:19:25,830
characterized by
respiratory and or

391
00:19:25,830 --> 00:19:28,930
cardiovascular
involvement and may occur

392
00:19:28,930 --> 00:19:31,990
without skin and
mucosa involvement.

393
00:19:32,810 --> 00:19:36,030
Anaphylaxis is likely
when any of the

394
00:19:36,030 --> 00:19:38,430
following three
criteria are fulfilled.

395
00:19:38,850 --> 00:19:42,570
No known allergen
exposure with sudden onset

396
00:19:42,570 --> 00:19:45,590
of an illness,
characterized as minutes

397
00:19:45,590 --> 00:19:49,070
to several hours,
with skin or mucosal

398
00:19:49,070 --> 00:19:52,990
involvement and either
respiratory or circulatory

399
00:19:52,990 --> 00:19:56,690
involvement. a likely
or known allergen

400
00:19:56,690 --> 00:19:59,790
exposure with sudden
onset of symptoms from

401
00:19:59,790 --> 00:20:04,270
two or more systems,
or a known allergen

402
00:20:04,270 --> 00:20:08,350
exposure with sudden onset
of either respiratory

403
00:20:08,350 --> 00:20:12,710
involvement after
exposure to a non-inhaled

404
00:20:12,710 --> 00:20:16,110
allergen or cardiovascular
involvement.

405
00:20:17,430 --> 00:20:20,490
Anaphylaxis epidemiology
shows a lifetime

406
00:20:20,490 --> 00:20:24,450
prevalence of 0
.3 to 5.1 percent.

407
00:20:24,590 --> 00:20:25,850
However,

408
00:20:26,450 --> 00:20:29,130
worrying data indicate
that recurrence

409
00:20:29,130 --> 00:20:34,490
of reactions occurs
in 26.5 up to 54

410
00:20:34,490 --> 00:20:37,430
percent of anaphylaxis
patients during

411
00:20:37,430 --> 00:20:41,010
a follow-up time
of 1.5 to 25 years.

412
00:20:41,270 --> 00:20:44,390
Therefore, it is
super important that

413
00:20:44,390 --> 00:20:47,130
all health care
providers can confidently

414
00:20:47,130 --> 00:20:49,990
identify and
manage anaphylaxis.

415
00:20:51,890 --> 00:20:55,530
Signs and symptoms
of anaphylaxis vary

416
00:20:55,530 --> 00:20:58,430
on an individual
basis, and common

417
00:20:58,430 --> 00:21:01,510
patient presentations
vary by patient age.

418
00:21:01,510 --> 00:21:04,330
This is not a
comprehensive list of

419
00:21:04,330 --> 00:21:07,470
potential signs by
system, but there are more

420
00:21:07,470 --> 00:21:10,550
common presentations
than others. It is

421
00:21:10,550 --> 00:21:13,310
very important to
note that not all

422
00:21:13,310 --> 00:21:16,450
anaphylactic reactions
have skin or mucosal

423
00:21:16,450 --> 00:21:19,010
involvement, although
it is very common.

424
00:21:21,390 --> 00:21:24,390
Medications, foods,
and venomous insects

425
00:21:24,390 --> 00:21:27,710
are the most common
causes of anaphylaxis.

426
00:21:28,450 --> 00:21:30,750
Antibiotics are the
most common cause

427
00:21:30,750 --> 00:21:34,050
of drug-induced
anaphylaxis. Some

428
00:21:34,050 --> 00:21:36,110
causes of
anaphylaxis are more

429
00:21:36,110 --> 00:21:38,750
situational, such
as allergy testing

430
00:21:38,750 --> 00:21:41,430
and allergen-specific
immunotherapy.

431
00:21:42,010 --> 00:21:44,750
Again, this is not
a comprehensive list

432
00:21:44,750 --> 00:21:49,040
of etiologies, just
more common ones. The

433
00:21:49,040 --> 00:21:51,570
primary mediators
released by mast cells

434
00:21:51,570 --> 00:21:54,350
during anaphylaxis
include histamine,

435
00:21:54,490 --> 00:21:56,450
prostaglandin D2,

436
00:21:56,690 --> 00:21:58,750
leukotriene D4,

437
00:21:59,250 --> 00:22:01,050
fleetly
activating factor,

438
00:22:01,050 --> 00:22:03,370
and preformed proteases.

439
00:22:03,930 --> 00:22:06,650
The major
pathophysiologic events

440
00:22:06,650 --> 00:22:08,970
caused by these
mediators include

441
00:22:08,970 --> 00:22:11,290
smooth muscle
spasm of bronchi,

442
00:22:11,630 --> 00:22:13,850
coronary arteries,
and the GI

443
00:22:13,850 --> 00:22:16,690
tract, as well as
increased vascular

444
00:22:16,690 --> 00:22:19,130
permeability,
vasodilation,

445
00:22:19,290 --> 00:22:21,830
stimulation of
sensory nerve endings

446
00:22:21,830 --> 00:22:24,710
with reflex activation
of vagal effector

447
00:22:24,710 --> 00:22:27,450
pathways, and
myocardial depression.

448
00:22:27,580 --> 00:22:30,010
These effects
result in your

449
00:22:30,010 --> 00:22:32,010
classic symptoms
of flushing,

450
00:22:32,290 --> 00:22:33,350
urticaria,

451
00:22:33,630 --> 00:22:34,730
angioedema,

452
00:22:35,010 --> 00:22:35,950
wheezing,

453
00:22:35,950 --> 00:22:39,030
decreased blood
pressure or hypotension,

454
00:22:39,570 --> 00:22:41,430
nausea, vomiting,

455
00:22:41,730 --> 00:22:42,430
diarrhea,

456
00:22:42,970 --> 00:22:44,410
uterine contractions,

457
00:22:44,410 --> 00:22:46,130
and myocardial ischemia.

458
00:22:48,990 --> 00:22:51,810
Cutaneous signs
and symptoms, such

459
00:22:51,810 --> 00:22:55,010
as angioedema and
urticaria, occur

460
00:22:55,010 --> 00:22:58,570
in 80-90% of
anaphylaxis cases.

461
00:22:59,750 --> 00:23:02,390
Angioedema is defined
as a localized

462
00:23:02,390 --> 00:23:05,530
subcutaneous or
submucosal swelling,

463
00:23:05,530 --> 00:23:08,090
which results
from extravasation

464
00:23:08,090 --> 00:23:11,150
of fluid into
interstitial tissues.

465
00:23:12,110 --> 00:23:14,030
Angioedema
typically affects

466
00:23:14,030 --> 00:23:15,870
areas with
loose connective

467
00:23:15,870 --> 00:23:19,010
tissue, such as
the face, lips,

468
00:23:19,010 --> 00:23:19,910
mouth,

469
00:23:20,130 --> 00:23:20,830
throat,

470
00:23:21,030 --> 00:23:25,130
larynx, uvula,
extremities, and genitalia.

471
00:23:25,950 --> 00:23:28,090
Angioedema can
be mast cell

472
00:23:28,090 --> 00:23:30,350
mediated, which is
what happens when

473
00:23:30,350 --> 00:23:32,500
it is a symptom
of anaphylaxis.

474
00:23:32,750 --> 00:23:33,930
However,

475
00:23:34,310 --> 00:23:37,110
angioedema can
also be bradykinin

476
00:23:37,110 --> 00:23:39,810
-mediated, with a
common trigger being

477
00:23:39,810 --> 00:23:43,390
angiotensin-converting
enzyme, or ACE,

478
00:23:43,430 --> 00:23:45,870
inhibitors, which
are a very common

479
00:23:45,870 --> 00:23:48,710
drug used in management
of hypertension

480
00:23:48,710 --> 00:23:51,370
and other cardiovascular
pathology.

481
00:23:52,230 --> 00:23:55,570
Urticaria is the
medical term for hives.

482
00:23:55,570 --> 00:23:58,650
A typical urticarial
lesion is an

483
00:23:58,650 --> 00:24:01,610
intensely pruritic
erythematous plaque.

484
00:24:01,830 --> 00:24:04,670
Any area of the body
can be affected.

485
00:24:04,810 --> 00:24:07,330
There are a
countless number of

486
00:24:07,330 --> 00:24:09,630
causes of urticaria,
ranging from

487
00:24:09,630 --> 00:24:12,510
anaphylaxis to
pressure to infection.

488
00:24:12,910 --> 00:24:15,710
It is important to
note that angioedema

489
00:24:15,710 --> 00:24:19,370
and urticaria may
occur in isolation or

490
00:24:19,370 --> 00:24:22,430
together in the
setting of anaphylaxis.

491
00:24:25,490 --> 00:24:27,870
Anaphylaxis
management is honestly

492
00:24:27,870 --> 00:24:29,810
simpler than
identifying it.

493
00:24:29,870 --> 00:24:32,370
Once you think
someone is having

494
00:24:32,370 --> 00:24:35,350
an anaphylactic
reaction, the first

495
00:24:35,350 --> 00:24:37,150
thing you should
do is remove

496
00:24:37,150 --> 00:24:39,590
exposure to the
allergen if possible.

497
00:24:39,890 --> 00:24:43,150
Then, assess the
patient's airway,

498
00:24:43,150 --> 00:24:46,990
breathing, circulation,
and mentation, and

499
00:24:46,990 --> 00:24:50,350
administer epinephrine.
I think everyone

500
00:24:50,350 --> 00:24:53,310
is familiar with some
version of an EpiPen,

501
00:24:53,310 --> 00:24:56,190
which is a transportable,
pre-measured

502
00:24:56,190 --> 00:24:58,970
vehicle for intramuscular
epinephrine.

503
00:24:59,170 --> 00:25:01,690
This should be
administered into the

504
00:25:01,690 --> 00:25:04,730
anterolateral thigh
for the quickest and

505
00:25:04,730 --> 00:25:07,050
greatest amount of
medication delivery.

506
00:25:07,230 --> 00:25:10,710
There is a new
epinephrine vehicle on

507
00:25:10,710 --> 00:25:13,150
the market, which
is an intranasal

508
00:25:13,150 --> 00:25:15,810
version of epinephrine,
which is packaged

509
00:25:15,810 --> 00:25:18,250
and handled the
same way as Narcan.

510
00:25:18,810 --> 00:25:20,570
Once epinephrine is

511
00:25:20,570 --> 00:25:23,830
administered, you
must call 911.

512
00:25:24,420 --> 00:25:26,590
All persons who have been

513
00:25:26,590 --> 00:25:29,530
administrated epinephrine
must go to the

514
00:25:29,530 --> 00:25:31,490
emergency room
for evaluation.

515
00:25:32,410 --> 00:25:34,010
I'm going to
say that again

516
00:25:34,010 --> 00:25:36,250
because it is
extremely important.

517
00:25:36,670 --> 00:25:38,870
All persons who have been

518
00:25:38,870 --> 00:25:42,250
administered epinephrine
must go to the

519
00:25:42,250 --> 00:25:44,830
emergency room
for evaluation.

520
00:25:45,810 --> 00:25:48,390
Once the call has
been made to emergency

521
00:25:48,390 --> 00:25:51,770
services, and if the
patient can tolerate it,

522
00:25:52,150 --> 00:25:54,730
have them lie supine
and elevate their

523
00:25:54,730 --> 00:25:57,290
lower extremities,
and continuously

524
00:25:57,290 --> 00:26:00,550
monitor their status
until EMS arrives.

525
00:26:01,150 --> 00:26:03,610
Now, I'm sure you
have a few questions,

526
00:26:03,610 --> 00:26:06,830
like why epinephrine?
How many times

527
00:26:06,830 --> 00:26:08,890
can you administer
epinephrine if

528
00:26:08,890 --> 00:26:11,170
symptoms don't
improve with one dose?

529
00:26:11,270 --> 00:26:13,490
Should you give a
patient anything

530
00:26:13,490 --> 00:26:16,290
alongside
epinephrine, or is

531
00:26:16,290 --> 00:26:18,130
epinephrine
alone sufficient?

532
00:26:18,580 --> 00:26:20,750
We are now going
to go over such

533
00:26:20,750 --> 00:26:22,570
details in the
following slides.

534
00:26:24,530 --> 00:26:28,070
A proper dose of
epinephrine is listed here

535
00:26:28,070 --> 00:26:31,090
on the slide and is
weight-based. In an

536
00:26:31,090 --> 00:26:34,450
emergency setting that
is not a hospital, the

537
00:26:34,450 --> 00:26:37,370
epinephrine you will
have access to will most

538
00:26:37,370 --> 00:26:39,790
likely be pre-dosed
in the form of an

539
00:26:39,790 --> 00:26:43,070
epinephrine auto-injector
or intranasal spray.

540
00:26:43,430 --> 00:26:45,870
But why do we
use epinephrine

541
00:26:45,870 --> 00:26:47,160
to treat anaphylaxis?

542
00:26:48,150 --> 00:26:51,330
Epinephrine is a non
-selective, direct

543
00:26:51,330 --> 00:26:54,810
sympathomimetic agent
with alpha-1, beta

544
00:26:54,810 --> 00:26:58,690
-1, and beta-2
effects. At low doses,

545
00:26:58,930 --> 00:27:01,350
beta-agonist
properties predominate,

546
00:27:01,350 --> 00:27:03,730
whereas at high
doses, alpha

547
00:27:03,730 --> 00:27:05,670
-agonist properties
predominate.

548
00:27:05,850 --> 00:27:08,330
In the setting
of anaphylaxis,

549
00:27:08,590 --> 00:27:11,590
epinephrine's alpha
effects are predominantly

550
00:27:11,590 --> 00:27:14,090
vascular smooth
muscle contraction.

551
00:27:14,670 --> 00:27:17,590
Epinephrine's beta
effects are increased

552
00:27:17,590 --> 00:27:20,270
heart rate and
increased myocardial

553
00:27:20,270 --> 00:27:23,950
contractility via
beta-1 receptors, and

554
00:27:23,950 --> 00:27:26,990
bronchodilation via
beta-2 receptors.

555
00:27:27,760 --> 00:27:29,830
So how many times can you

556
00:27:29,830 --> 00:27:31,550
give a patient
epinephrine?

557
00:27:31,810 --> 00:27:35,430
You can administer another
dose of epinephrine

558
00:27:35,430 --> 00:27:39,370
every 5 to 15 minutes,
with most patients

559
00:27:39,370 --> 00:27:43,990
responding to 1, 2,
or at most 3 doses.

560
00:27:44,290 --> 00:27:47,510
However, if symptoms
do not resolve,

561
00:27:47,510 --> 00:27:50,070
involved, then an
intravenous infusion

562
00:27:50,070 --> 00:27:51,990
of epinephrine
may be warranted.

563
00:27:52,570 --> 00:27:54,650
I have another
question for you.

564
00:27:55,050 --> 00:27:57,790
Are there any
contraindications to using

565
00:27:57,790 --> 00:28:00,170
epinephrine in the
case of anaphylaxis?

566
00:28:01,530 --> 00:28:03,150
The answer is no.

567
00:28:03,730 --> 00:28:05,550
Give all patients with

568
00:28:05,550 --> 00:28:07,350
anaphylaxis epinephrine.

569
00:28:07,650 --> 00:28:09,090
It's that simple.

570
00:28:10,270 --> 00:28:12,990
So what other medications
should you give

571
00:28:12,990 --> 00:28:16,210
in the setting of
anaphylaxis, if any?

572
00:28:19,170 --> 00:28:21,790
There's always a
question of, should

573
00:28:21,790 --> 00:28:24,470
I give an H1
antihistamine like

574
00:28:24,470 --> 00:28:26,730
Cetirazine or
Benadryl, which is

575
00:28:26,730 --> 00:28:28,310
the brand name for
diphenhydramine?

576
00:28:28,920 --> 00:28:30,870
The short answer is no.

577
00:28:31,010 --> 00:28:32,970
The long answer is maybe.

578
00:28:33,510 --> 00:28:35,690
I know you probably
just rolled your

579
00:28:35,690 --> 00:28:37,750
eyes at me, but it
really is maybe.

580
00:28:38,850 --> 00:28:40,470
Antihistamines
can provide some

581
00:28:40,470 --> 00:28:41,890
relief of symptoms like

582
00:28:41,890 --> 00:28:44,270
urticaria and
associated paritis.

583
00:28:44,730 --> 00:28:45,530
However,

584
00:28:45,710 --> 00:28:48,170
they should never be
used alone to treat

585
00:28:48,170 --> 00:28:51,030
anaphylaxis, because
they have no effect

586
00:28:51,030 --> 00:28:53,770
on upper or lower
airway obstruction,

587
00:28:53,910 --> 00:28:56,350
shock, or hypotension.

588
00:28:56,550 --> 00:28:58,710
If you decide
to administer

589
00:28:58,710 --> 00:28:59,970
an antihistamine,

590
00:29:00,190 --> 00:29:03,310
aim for a second
-generation antihistamine

591
00:29:03,310 --> 00:29:06,050
to decrease the risk
of sedative effects.

592
00:29:06,710 --> 00:29:08,890
How about H2
antihistamines

593
00:29:08,890 --> 00:29:09,950
like famotidine?

594
00:29:10,230 --> 00:29:12,490
Some studies
suggest patients

595
00:29:12,490 --> 00:29:14,810
may benefit from
symptom improvement,

596
00:29:14,810 --> 00:29:16,390
but research is limited.

597
00:29:17,250 --> 00:29:19,070
Another common question

598
00:29:19,070 --> 00:29:20,810
is, do steroids help?

599
00:29:21,290 --> 00:29:23,090
The answer is no.

600
00:29:23,890 --> 00:29:26,310
Leukocorticoids do
not have an active

601
00:29:26,310 --> 00:29:29,130
role in the treatment
of anaphylaxis due

602
00:29:29,130 --> 00:29:31,770
to their delay in
onset of action, and

603
00:29:31,770 --> 00:29:34,510
there is no rigorously
conducted published

604
00:29:34,510 --> 00:29:37,090
literature that
supports their use

605
00:29:37,090 --> 00:29:40,070
in this setting.
There is a theoretical

606
00:29:40,070 --> 00:29:42,310
rationale for their
use in preventing

607
00:29:42,310 --> 00:29:46,390
biphasic reactions,
but this is not true.

608
00:29:47,170 --> 00:29:49,130
Glucocorticoids do not

609
00:29:49,130 --> 00:29:51,190
prevent biphasic
reactions.

610
00:29:51,250 --> 00:29:54,630
The only supported
use of glucocorticoids

611
00:29:54,630 --> 00:29:56,310
in the setting
of anaphylaxis

612
00:29:56,310 --> 00:29:58,850
is for residual
bronchospasm

613
00:29:58,850 --> 00:30:01,550
that is not responsive
to albuterol.

614
00:30:04,370 --> 00:30:07,570
Other adjuvant measures
you will more commonly

615
00:30:07,570 --> 00:30:10,690
see utilized in the
hospital when it

616
00:30:10,690 --> 00:30:13,400
comes to anaphylaxis
management include

617
00:30:13,400 --> 00:30:16,650
albuterol to manage
bronchospasm, since it is

618
00:30:16,650 --> 00:30:19,970
a beta-2 agonist, and
supplemental oxygen

619
00:30:19,970 --> 00:30:23,550
if the patient is
hypoxic. In more severe

620
00:30:23,550 --> 00:30:26,650
cases of hypotension,
which are refractory

621
00:30:26,650 --> 00:30:29,490
to intramuscular or
intranasal epinephrine,

622
00:30:29,950 --> 00:30:32,190
isotonic fluids
may be administered

623
00:30:32,190 --> 00:30:35,650
alongside an epinephrine
infusion and placement

624
00:30:35,650 --> 00:30:38,110
of the patient in
Trendelenburg position

625
00:30:38,110 --> 00:30:40,110
in hopes of raising
blood pressure.

626
00:30:40,470 --> 00:30:43,390
Some hospital
protocols may include

627
00:30:43,390 --> 00:30:45,930
use of other
vasopressors or glucagon.

628
00:30:46,510 --> 00:30:49,270
Glucagon is commonly
given to patients who

629
00:30:49,270 --> 00:30:52,290
take beta blockers
regularly because they are

630
00:30:52,290 --> 00:30:55,090
more likely to not
respond to epinephrine.

631
00:30:58,030 --> 00:31:01,110
All patients with
anaphylaxis should be

632
00:31:01,110 --> 00:31:04,030
observed for at least
four to six hours in

633
00:31:04,030 --> 00:31:07,070
the emergency department
because a late-phase

634
00:31:07,070 --> 00:31:09,590
reaction is most
likely to occur in this

635
00:31:09,590 --> 00:31:12,400
time frame if it is
going to occur at all.

636
00:31:12,400 --> 00:31:16,230
The risk of biphasic
reaction is increased

637
00:31:16,230 --> 00:31:18,450
in patients with
moderate to severe

638
00:31:18,450 --> 00:31:22,110
anaphylaxis who did not
respond initially to

639
00:31:22,110 --> 00:31:25,170
epinephrine and required
more than one dose.

640
00:31:25,990 --> 00:31:27,710
These are the
patients who should be

641
00:31:27,710 --> 00:31:29,710
admitted for
prolonged observation.

642
00:31:30,450 --> 00:31:33,450
Other potential reasons
for longer observation

643
00:31:33,450 --> 00:31:36,310
periods include history
of severe asthma

644
00:31:36,310 --> 00:31:39,490
or wheezing, ingestion
of the antigen,

645
00:31:39,490 --> 00:31:42,770
and potential for
continued absorption, and

646
00:31:42,770 --> 00:31:45,790
of course, history of
biphasic reactions.

647
00:31:46,010 --> 00:31:48,730
For the remaining
patient population who

648
00:31:48,730 --> 00:31:51,950
respond well to one dose
of epinephrine, they

649
00:31:51,950 --> 00:31:54,170
can be discharged in
four to six hours.

650
00:31:56,270 --> 00:31:58,270
When it is time
for discharge

651
00:31:58,270 --> 00:32:00,050
planning for a
patient who presented

652
00:32:00,050 --> 00:32:02,590
with anaphylaxis,
there are a few

653
00:32:02,590 --> 00:32:04,450
key points that
must be addressed.

654
00:32:04,750 --> 00:32:07,830
First, and most
importantly, these

655
00:32:07,830 --> 00:32:10,170
patients must be
discharged with some

656
00:32:10,170 --> 00:32:13,180
vehicle of epinephrine
for self-administration

657
00:32:13,180 --> 00:32:15,890
and or a
prescription for one.

658
00:32:15,910 --> 00:32:19,090
This is usually an
auto-injector, and

659
00:32:19,090 --> 00:32:21,350
patients must be
counseled on how to

660
00:32:21,350 --> 00:32:24,150
use it and how to
store it properly.

661
00:32:24,590 --> 00:32:27,050
Patients should
also establish an

662
00:32:27,050 --> 00:32:29,490
anaphylaxis action
plan if they

663
00:32:29,490 --> 00:32:31,290
do not yet have
one established.

664
00:32:31,690 --> 00:32:33,850
This plan should
include both

665
00:32:33,850 --> 00:32:37,090
recognition and
treatment of anaphylaxis

666
00:32:37,090 --> 00:32:40,070
in layperson
terminology and in

667
00:32:40,070 --> 00:32:41,930
the patient's
preferred language.

668
00:32:41,930 --> 00:32:45,010
Just so you know,
there is a general

669
00:32:45,010 --> 00:32:47,350
anaphylaxis
emergency care plan

670
00:32:47,350 --> 00:32:51,050
available in both English
and Spanish online

671
00:32:51,050 --> 00:32:53,170
through the Food
Allergy Research

672
00:32:53,170 --> 00:32:56,510
and Education, or
FAIR, program that

673
00:32:56,510 --> 00:32:58,590
can be printed if
you ever need one.

674
00:32:59,210 --> 00:33:02,930
education on allergen
avoidance and hidden

675
00:33:02,930 --> 00:33:06,290
allergens like cross
-contamination beauty

676
00:33:06,290 --> 00:33:08,970
products and cooking
methods as well as

677
00:33:08,970 --> 00:33:11,470
substances with a known
incidence of cross

678
00:33:11,470 --> 00:33:13,930
-reactivity with a
given allergen should

679
00:33:13,930 --> 00:33:18,050
be delivered in the
same manner finally you

680
00:33:18,050 --> 00:33:19,930
should emphasize
the importance of

681
00:33:19,930 --> 00:33:22,910
outpatient follow-up with
a primary care provider

682
00:33:22,910 --> 00:33:26,570
and an allergist
especially if anaphylaxis

683
00:33:26,570 --> 00:33:29,110
occurred due to an
unknown allergen.

684
00:33:29,680 --> 00:33:32,470
A nice summary that
can be used when

685
00:33:32,470 --> 00:33:34,770
counseling patients
can be remembered

686
00:33:34,770 --> 00:33:36,990
with the mnemonic
SAFE, which

687
00:33:36,990 --> 00:33:38,710
describes the steps
that need to be

688
00:33:38,710 --> 00:33:40,890
completed once patients
are discharged.

689
00:33:41,190 --> 00:33:44,190
SAFE stands for
seek support,

690
00:33:44,390 --> 00:33:47,410
allergen identification
and avoidance,

691
00:33:47,570 --> 00:33:49,870
follow-up for
specialty care,

692
00:33:49,870 --> 00:33:52,350
and epinephrine
for emergencies.

693
00:33:54,730 --> 00:33:57,410
We are now going to
switch gears a bit

694
00:33:57,410 --> 00:34:00,070
to talk about the
other general class of

695
00:34:00,070 --> 00:34:02,270
immediate hypersensitivity
reactions,

696
00:34:02,590 --> 00:34:03,870
atopic diseases.

697
00:34:04,890 --> 00:34:06,930
Atopy refers
to the genetic

698
00:34:06,930 --> 00:34:09,630
tendency to develop
atopic diseases,

699
00:34:09,630 --> 00:34:11,770
such as allergic
rhinitis,

700
00:34:11,970 --> 00:34:12,790
asthma,

701
00:34:13,170 --> 00:34:14,730
atopic dermatitis,

702
00:34:14,970 --> 00:34:17,390
angioedema,
and urticaria.

703
00:34:17,790 --> 00:34:20,670
Atopy is typically
associated with

704
00:34:20,670 --> 00:34:22,310
heightened immune
responses to

705
00:34:22,310 --> 00:34:25,310
common allergens,
especially inhaled

706
00:34:25,310 --> 00:34:27,190
allergens and
food allergens.

707
00:34:27,930 --> 00:34:29,690
Atopy can
sometimes run in

708
00:34:29,690 --> 00:34:31,870
families, and
you may hear this

709
00:34:31,870 --> 00:34:34,190
referred to as
the atopic trait.

710
00:34:34,590 --> 00:34:37,330
Atopy affects a
significant portion

711
00:34:37,330 --> 00:34:39,810
of the global
population, with a

712
00:34:39,810 --> 00:34:42,370
prevalence estimated
between 10 and

713
00:34:42,370 --> 00:34:44,650
30 percent in
developed countries.

714
00:34:45,210 --> 00:34:48,430
Atopic children
commonly develop signs

715
00:34:48,430 --> 00:34:50,950
and symptoms in a
predictable sequence,

716
00:34:50,950 --> 00:34:54,210
called the allergic
march or atopic march.

717
00:34:54,250 --> 00:34:57,790
The most common temporal
progression of such

718
00:34:57,790 --> 00:35:01,610
diagnoses is atopic
dermatitis in infancy,

719
00:35:01,930 --> 00:35:04,170
food allergy
as a toddler,

720
00:35:04,450 --> 00:35:07,630
then allergic rhinitis
and asthma in elementary

721
00:35:07,630 --> 00:35:10,350
school, but these
can present later in

722
00:35:10,350 --> 00:35:13,650
childhood. We are now
going to talk about

723
00:35:13,650 --> 00:35:16,530
each of these atopic
diseases in more detail.

724
00:35:16,960 --> 00:35:20,490
Before we do so, I
want to point out that

725
00:35:20,490 --> 00:35:23,090
atopy and allergy
are commonly used

726
00:35:23,090 --> 00:35:25,830
interchangeably, but
they are different.

727
00:35:26,270 --> 00:35:29,010
Allergy is defined
as immediate

728
00:35:29,010 --> 00:35:34,070
hypersensitivity mediated
by IgE, but atopy is

729
00:35:34,070 --> 00:35:37,710
the genetic tendency
to develop allergy.

730
00:35:40,090 --> 00:35:43,390
Atopic dermatitis,
also known as eczema,

731
00:35:43,390 --> 00:35:46,550
is a chronic, pruritic,
which means itchy,

732
00:35:46,770 --> 00:35:48,630
inflammatory
skin disease.

733
00:35:48,750 --> 00:35:51,310
It originally presents as

734
00:35:51,310 --> 00:35:53,190
erythematous
papules and vesicles

735
00:35:53,190 --> 00:35:55,130
with exudation
and crusting.

736
00:35:55,330 --> 00:35:58,590
It then progresses
to dry, scaly, or

737
00:35:58,590 --> 00:36:01,370
excoriated papules
or skin thickenings.

738
00:36:02,050 --> 00:36:04,490
Pruritus is more
pronounced at night and

739
00:36:04,490 --> 00:36:06,970
is exacerbated by
irritants such as wool.

740
00:36:07,310 --> 00:36:08,750
and may occur following

741
00:36:08,750 --> 00:36:11,010
ingestion of
allergenic foods.

742
00:36:11,350 --> 00:36:14,260
Scratching and rubbing
cause the typical

743
00:36:14,260 --> 00:36:16,690
dermatological skin
eruption to flare.

744
00:36:16,890 --> 00:36:18,510
There is often a strong

745
00:36:18,510 --> 00:36:20,450
family history of atopy.

746
00:36:20,890 --> 00:36:23,650
The distribution of
lesions varies with

747
00:36:23,650 --> 00:36:26,490
age, with the
forehead, cheeks, and

748
00:36:26,490 --> 00:36:29,610
extensor surfaces of
limbs commonly affected

749
00:36:29,610 --> 00:36:32,710
in early childhood,
which then shifts

750
00:36:32,710 --> 00:36:35,510
to flexural areas,
such as the creases

751
00:36:35,510 --> 00:36:37,850
of elbows and knees
in adolescents,

752
00:36:37,890 --> 00:36:40,390
and adults may
develop eczema

753
00:36:40,390 --> 00:36:42,030
on their hands as well.

754
00:36:42,370 --> 00:36:45,590
Eczema affects
about 15 to 30

755
00:36:45,590 --> 00:36:48,330
percent of children
and 2 to 10

756
00:36:48,330 --> 00:36:50,310
percent of
adults worldwide.

757
00:36:53,150 --> 00:36:56,650
In patients with
highly pigmented skin,

758
00:36:56,790 --> 00:37:00,310
dry skin may appear
whitish or gray,

759
00:37:00,730 --> 00:37:03,350
erythema may appear
violaceous or

760
00:37:03,350 --> 00:37:06,510
may not be visible
at all, and thickened

761
00:37:06,510 --> 00:37:08,850
areas may appear
hyperpigmented.

762
00:37:09,510 --> 00:37:12,710
Treatment of atopic
dermatitis includes

763
00:37:12,710 --> 00:37:15,810
proper skin care,
environmental control,

764
00:37:16,190 --> 00:37:18,070
allergen avoidance, and

765
00:37:18,070 --> 00:37:19,590
often pharmaceuticals.

766
00:37:19,910 --> 00:37:22,450
The most effective
preventive measure

767
00:37:22,450 --> 00:37:25,410
is non-irritating
topical lubricants.

768
00:37:25,690 --> 00:37:28,090
Topical steroids
are effective

769
00:37:28,090 --> 00:37:30,370
for milder skin
involvement.

770
00:37:30,490 --> 00:37:33,710
For moderate to severe
eczema, there are

771
00:37:33,710 --> 00:37:36,010
several biologics
that are options if

772
00:37:36,010 --> 00:37:38,730
topical medications
fail to resolve flares.

773
00:37:39,210 --> 00:37:41,430
Frequent bathing
and use of

774
00:37:41,430 --> 00:37:43,730
irritating detergents,
lotions, and

775
00:37:43,730 --> 00:37:45,710
